stations  over a sampling of days and time so they  just kind of stalked everyone who  touched those dispensers and they did  find that people did use the sunscreen  so it was definitely better than not  having the dispensers there but they did  still find that people didn't use enough  sunscreen and on days where it was  cloudy even though the UV index was  still quite High people used the  sunscreen a lot less so yeah I think  having these free sunscreen dispensers  definitely helps we have them at a lot  of beaches in Australia we also have  paid sunscreen dispenses as well where  you pay like I don't know a dollar and  you get a pretty decent dollop and I  think this is a really good Public  Health investment because the sort of  intense sunburn exposure that you get at  the beach is one of the biggest  contributors to skin cancer but at the  same time I don't know if it's really  about free versus not free I think it is  more of a convenience thing for a lot of  people sunscreen doesn't cost that much  in Australia we have supermarkets that  sell really good sunscreen for about two  dollars I think it's more about having  the sunscreen there at the point where  you realize you forgot to bring your  sunscreen I think a lot of us are just  lazy a lot of the time we don't do  things that are good for us because it  is more effort there is also this other  effect where sometimes people don't  appreciate something that's free so it  might be useful while it's novel but  then they're less invested in it so they  might not use it all the time loss  aversion is usually a more powerful  motivator than games so if we buy a  sunscreen and it's going to expire in a  certain amount time then we are probably  going to use it so it doesn't go to  waste whereas if we got a free sunscreen  we might not actually use it because we  haven't lost anything we got given this  free sunscreen it was a bonus to begin  with we're not losing anything by not  using it so it would be really  interesting to have a study to see what  happens if you give people free tubes of  sunscreen for everyday use versus making  them pay two dollars for it I think if  sunscreens in general were cheaper  although not necessarily free then  people would definitely use it more  there are a lot of people who try to  save on more expensive products and  justify that and this is one of the  reasons why when I do sunscreen reviews  I try to make it clear that the best  sunscreen is one that you can use a lot  of regularly and so that is about  texture but also about budget so I hope  you enjoyed this chatty video let me  know what you think I feel like these  ones weren't very controversial maybe  they were I don't know maybe I'll get  flamed in the comments but yeah if you  have unpopular opinions please leave  them below and I might respond to the  them in a future video like And  subscribe more videos should be popping  up around me if you want to nerd out  more
 

